    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d83">

                <pb facs="0262" n="234" xml:id="pg234"/>

                <head>83. Telegram From the Department of State to the Mission at the United
                        Nations<anchor n="1" xml:id="d83-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1955-11-01T18:26:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">November 1,
                                1955—6:26 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>Gadel 76. Re radiation (Delga 175).<anchor n="2" xml:id="d83-f2"/> USDEL can make
                    following statement on moratorium: “The US believes that a moratorium on tests
                    of nuclear weapons is an inseparable part of the disarmament problem and cannot
                    be dealt with alone. Any agreed disarmament program must be predicated on
                    equitable and effective inspection and control systems applicable to all
                    elements of this agreed disarmament program. This moratorium question is,
                    consequently, properly part of the efforts to reach agreement on disarmament
                    carried on in the UN Disarmament Commission, its Subcommittee and other
                    areas.”</p>
                <p>Foregoing is maximum we believe USDEL could say on this problem now in light
                    present stage disarmament review.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">Hoover</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d83-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d83-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Department of State, Central Files, 700.5611/10–2855. Confidential;
                    Priority. Drafted by Meyers and approved by Bond.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d83-f2" n="2" xml:id="d83-f2-content">
                <p>Delga 175 from New York, October 28, indicated that the problem of a moratorium
                    on nuclear tests would probably arise during the forthcoming debate on radiation
                    and asked for instructions. Since the Soviets had urged cessation of tests in
                    their May 10 proposals as well as in their suggested amendments to the radiation
                    resolution, Lodge said it would be useful if he could say that the United States
                    supported cessation of tests as part of a comprehensive disarmament system. (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Ibid</hi>.)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
